    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": true, "note": "The sign ('upregulates'/'downregulates') is a literal column, so sign prediction has its label in place; edge existence would need negatives sampled from the 1.09M possible source-target pairs, which is construction but only within one table."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": false, "note": "Only NCBI Gene IDs and gene symbols are in the files. Any model input (protein sequence, promoter sequence, expression profile) has to be fetched from a second database for all 1,559 transcription factors - a small, easy lookup, so this is not what kills it."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "z_score and p_value are named and dimensionless; 25% of p-values are exactly 0 (numerical underflow from the Z test), which is a tie-handling nuisance, not missing units."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": true, "n_rows": 131181, "note": "131,181 signed edges verified by counting rows in the two filtered edge CSVs; far above the 1,500-row bar."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~20 MB of CSV; ~0.5 GB in memory with ESM-2 small-checkpoint embeddings for 1,559 proteins", "note": "Trivially laptop-sized; verified the unzipped CSVs total under 20 MB."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "67,650 + 63,531 = 131,181 edges and 1,559 nodes exactly match the abstract and the download manifest; unfiltered 373,250 also matches. Only the website blurb swaps the source/target counts (files show 700 sources, 1,559 targets)."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": false, "note": "KILLER. Every edge, its sign and its z-score are outputs of a computational pipeline: ChEA3 (a transcription factor enrichment tool built on ChIP-seq and co-expression libraries) run over RummaGEO auto-generated differential-expression gene sets, counted, then tested against shuffled networks. No row traces to a measurement that 'TF X regulates TF Y'. Training on it distils ChEA3 plus RummaGEO, with ChEA3's unknown error rate as the ceiling - exactly the circularity A8 describes. The paper itself says confirming these edges would require in vitro and in vivo testing."
  },
